WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:06.780
Hey, Kelly, it's Sue.

2
00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:10.980
I'm the voice in the hands
behind the Knit It Now videos.

3
00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.940
You took time to write to me today about

4
00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:18.260
a cast-on method that you're
using and you're unhappy with.

5
00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:21.260
Let me read to you exactly what you said.

6
00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:26.580
"Every time I start to knit and put
the comb on, the comb pulls stitches down,

7
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:30.940
so there are big holes,
and the comb falls off the stitches."

8
00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:35.980
It took me a minute to figure out what it
is that was happening,

9
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.740
and when you told me that you were using
an LK-150 machine, the light bulb came on.

10
00:00:42.770 --> 00:00:47.480
Looking at the LK 150 manual,
this is the cast on that they tell you.

11
00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:48.860
They teach you how to use.

12
00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:51.300
And that's what it looks like.

13
00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:52.380
It's not you.

14
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:53.740
You didn't do anything wrong.

15
00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:55.780
You don't have the wrong cast on comb.

16
00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:57.780
It's just ugly.

17
00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:03.380
This is probably what
you're seeing right?Now

18
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:05.020
there's nothing wrong with this cast-on.

19
00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:06.820
It's a closed cast-on.

20
00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:11.100
The stitches are not going to run,
and it has lots and lots of uses.

21
00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:14.380
And again, the key to this cast-on is fast.

22
00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:16.490
So if you're going to scrap on,

23
00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:21.620
you're knitting a swatch, you don't
need to have a nice pretty finish.

24
00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:26.480
This one's perfect,
and we use it all the time.

25
00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:31.210
But since you don't like the appearance of
it, you need to learn a different cast-on.

26
00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:35.740
Now, the one I would recommend you
to start with is an e-wrap cast on.

27
00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:38.620
E-wrap cast-on is very easy to do.

28
00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:43.290
It's stretchy, and it has a nice finish.

29
00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.900
So I recommend that you take a look in our

30
00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:48.420
Learning Library and search
for cast on methods.

31
00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.660
There's a number of them there.

32
00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:53.620
Part of your learning journey is to master

33
00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:58.930
a number of techniques and figure out
when it is that you would use them.

34
00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:04.260
Now, the next step on your journey from
casting on would be to putting a hem.

35
00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:07.850
Now, I'm reading into your message
that you didn't like this ugly finish

36
00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.300
because you wanted to start
a scarf or a blanket.

37
00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:13.730
And you've got this ugly all
these loops hanging right?

38
00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:19.580
The next thing you're going to need
to learn is hems. In machine knitting

39
00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:21.500
we want to finish that edge.

40
00:02:21.530 --> 00:02:23.780
So you've got a pretty beginning to your

41
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:27.220
scarf or your blanket or your sweater
or whatever it is you're knitting.

42
00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:33.560
So the next thing to look
for in the learning library are hems.

43
00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:37.420
We also have a Home Study Course

44
00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:43.540
for absolute beginners that covers
both hems and casting on.

45
00:02:43.570 --> 00:02:49.700
And I believe that information in there
would be very valuable to you as well.

46
00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:52.740
So, again, thanks for writing.

47
00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:54.660
Try some different techniques.

48
00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:55.900
You aren't doing anything wrong.

49
00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:58.440
and happy knitting.

